On 2007-09-05, Carl <carlhnelson@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Sep 5, 3:44 pm, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
>> On 2007-09-05, Carl <carlhnel...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> There's currently no barrier in vanilla to early wishing for
>> crossaligned quest artifacts and saving them until conversion post-Quest.
>
> But the time of wishing isn't the issue.  It's when someone can _use_
> the artifact that is important.

The circumstance already exists in vanilla where people can use other
classes' Quest artifacts prior to completing their own Quest.

The reason to wish for a crossaligned artifact early, even though
one couldn't use it, is to circumvent the artifact count -- where
post-Quest and post-conversion, artifacts may have been generated
already and render it too costly in wishes to be worth trying for. 

>> A low-level neutral priest can wish for the Eye anyway.  If this
>> circumstance isn't broken, then converting pre-quest (with the attendant
>> difficulty of now having to kill your Quest Leader _as well as_ your
>> Quest Nemesis) and wishing for the Eye _also_ isn't broken.
>
> I see your point that there is a trade-off for such an early game
> maneuver.  Note that I never used the word "broken".

It's not that there's a tradeoff; it's that the game currently contains
the imbalance you're worried about.

I realize that you didn't use the word "broken", but that's what we're
dealing with here.  Imbalances are part of the game; some roles can do
things others can't, some classes can do things others can't.  An
imbalance is only a problem if it creates a circumstance where the game
is broken.

Allowing players to convert pre-Quest does not break the game any more
than the game is already broken.

>> If a neutral priest with 41HP wishing for the Eye _is_ broken, on the
>> other hand, then a chaotic priest converting pre-quest isn't going to
>> be any more broken.
>
> I never said that either was broken, I'm saying that (in Vanilla) if
> you want to play an elven priest then you may get a nice shiny bell or
> a flute, but you won't be getting an early Eye of the Aethiopica.

Comparing a wooden flute to the Eye of the Aethiopica is disingenuous in
the extreme, almost to the point of silly.

There is no practical difference between an elven priest with an 
early-game Eye and a human priest with an early-game Eye... except in
Spork, the elven priest is going to have to kill his Quest Leader as
well.

Given that the Quest Leaders are going to be (hopefully) tough as 
nails, this is _not_ something that should be considered the 'easy'
way to go, and the Eye is not likely to be enough to make up for it.

> More generally, what I am getting at, is that by allowing the pre-
> quest use of a cross-class&alignment artifact, the trade-offs inherent
> to one's choice of race/class/alignment are minimized.

What tradeoffs, specifically, are you talking about that would be minimized?

Let's take sleep resistance as an elven priest for an example.  In
vanilla, elves get sleep resistance and humans can wish for the Eye.

This comparison isn't meaningful.  The Eye vastly outranks free sleep
resistance; thus, the human priest is head-and-shoulders better than his
elven counterpart in vanilla.

Now by extension, if it's broken to allow the elf to wish for the Eye, 
it is also broken to allow the human to wish for the Eye.  If the
Eye is this much of a game-breaker, then wishing for the Eye is the
problem -- not whether someone can convert pre-quest to obtain it.

> By the way, if you do allow pre-quest conversion, what alignment 
> will you make the Quest artifact?

It will remain your starting alignment, and will blast you every time you
try to use it.  Self-conversion is a bad thing, remember; it'll just no
longer be an automatic game-ender.

